# Working Backwards in Problem-Solving

Working backwards—also called "taking the problem as solved"—is a heuristic where you start from the desired outcome and reason backward to the given information. Instead of moving from data to conclusion, you invert the direction: assume the solution is known and ask what must be true for it to hold.

Polya describes this as one of the most powerful strategies in the "devising a plan" phase. It is particularly useful when the path from given to unknown is unclear, but the structure of the solution is more transparent.

This technique connects to [[real-options-analysis]], which treats decisions as flexible options and reasons about future states before committing. It also relates to [[scenario-planning]], where you construct plausible futures and work backward to identify what actions would be required.
